  "prediction": "**Apple's Decision to Remove Headphone Jack Sparks Outrage**\n\nAt the recent Apple keynote event, the company announced the removal of the standard 3.5mm aux cable input, also known as the headphone jack, from its latest iPhone 7 model. Marketing chief Phil Schiller explained the decision by stating that it was a matter of \"courage\" to move on and do something better for all users. However, his words were met with widespread mockery and criticism on social media.\n\nCritics argue that Apple's decision is not about innovation or progress, but rather about profit and control. The removal of the headphone jack forces users to purchase expensive and unwieldy dongles or wireless headphones, such as Apple's AirPods, which cost $159. This decision has been seen as a move to further Apple's walled garden approach, where users are forced to use proprietary products and services.\n\nMany experts and users have pointed out that there is no technological excuse for removing the headphone jack, as music does not sound better over a Lightning cable or wireless technology. In fact, wired audio is often preferred for its superior sound quality. The decision has been seen as a step backward for Apple, which has traditionally been known for its user-friendly and seamless experience.\n\nThe removal of the headphone jack has also had a ripple effect on other devices that use the standard 3.5mm connector, such as Square's credit card dongle, temperature sensors, light meters, thermal imaging cameras, and personal breathalyzers. These devices will now need to be refurbished with a proprietary Lightning connector, adding to the cost and inconvenience for users.\n\nThe decision has sparked a large amount of outrage and criticism, with many users expressing their frustration and disappointment on social media. The old Apple mantra of \"it just works\" is now officially dead, as users are forced to navigate a more complex and proprietary ecosystem.\n\nIn conclusion, Apple's decision to remove the headphone jack from its latest iPhone model has been met with widespread criticism and outrage. The move has been seen as a profit-driven decision that prioritizes Apple's interests over user convenience and experience. As the company continues to push its walled garden approach, users are left to wonder what other features and functionalities will be sacrificed in the name of innovation and progress.",
